RECNO Design Rationale
=======================

This document consolidates architectural design decisions for the RECNO
table access method.  It covers MVCC correctness, WAL record format, and
lock ordering.  The intended audience is
PostgreSQL committers evaluating the code for review.


1. MVCC Correctness
====================

1.1 Visibility model
---------------------

RECNO uses heap-compatible xmin/xmax MVCC.  Each tuple header carries the
inserter XID (t_xmin) and the deleter/updater XID (t_xmax, physically in
the low 32 bits of the t_commit_ts word).  A tuple is visible to a snapshot
iff its t_xmin is committed-and-visible to the snapshot and its t_xmax is
invalid, not committed, or not visible -- exactly like heap's
HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC.  Commit visibility comes from CLOG.  The single
visibility function is RecnoTupleSatisfiesMVCC(), reached via
RecnoTupleVisibleToSnapshotDual() from every read site.

The RECNO_TUPLE_UNCOMMITTED flag is a hint bit set at insert/delete/update
and cleared at commit; it no longer drives read visibility (CLOG does).
While it is set, the sLog is consulted (SLogTupleLookup /
SLogTupleGetDirtyXid) for self-visibility and command-level (CID) checks
and to serialize write-write conflicts, but the authoritative commit oracle
is CLOG.

The pre-commit "dirty read" window closes for free: a reader resolves
t_xmin against CLOG, which reports committed only at the durability point
(RecordTransactionCommit flushes the commit record).  A not-yet-committed
(hence not-yet-durable) inserter is simply invisible -- no timestamp
compare, no durable side-map.

1.2 In-place UPDATE and version reconstruction
-----------------------------------------------

An in-place UPDATE keeps the newest version on the page (new t_xmin) and
pushes the old version to the per-relation UNDO fork (zheap-style) via the
tuple's version pointer (t_verptr).  A snapshot that cannot see the
updater's xmin reads the old version back from the fork with
RecnoReconstructVisibleVersion() (recno_pvs.c), which walks t_verptr and
stops at the version whose producing xid is visible (XidInMVCCSnapshot).

Committed in-place UPDATE markers are dropped from the sLog at commit
(WS-PVS3); the durable UNDO-fork chain is what serves an older snapshot.

1.3 Isolation levels
---------------------

  - Read Committed: fresh snapshot per statement (standard MVCC snapshot).
  - Repeatable Read: single snapshot for the whole transaction.
  - Serializable: full SSI via the core predicate-locking infrastructure
    (predicate.c).  RECNO acquires SIREAD predicate locks on scanned tuples
    (PredicateLockTID) and reports rw-antidependencies via
    CheckForSerializableConflictIn/Out.  Dangerous-structure detection
    (two rw-antidependencies into a pivot) is performed by the core,
    identical to heap.

The write-write conflict probe RecnoTupleHasCommittedUpdateAfter() reads
the UNDO-fork head verptr's urec_xid and resolves it against CLOG.  That is
lost-update / serialization-conflict detection (the same concern for which
heap consults CLOG in HeapTupleSatisfiesUpdate), NOT snapshot read
visibility.

1.4 Pruning and retention safety
---------------------------------

VACUUM/prune reclamation uses an XID horizon, exactly like heap.
RecnoGetOldestXminHorizon() returns GetOldestNonRemovableTransactionId();
a committed-deleted tuple whose deleter XID (t_xmax) precedes that horizon
is invisible to every current and future snapshot and can be removed
(RecnoTupleDeadToAll).  Retained sLog before-images are freed by
SLogTupleCleanupRetained(), which gates on the same XID horizon.


2. WAL Record Format
=====================

RECNO registers as resource manager RM_RECNO_ID.  The REDO entry point
is recno_redo().  All records use REGBUF_STANDARD for automatic FPI
support.

2.1 Record Types
-----------------

  XLOG_RECNO_INSERT           (0x00)  New tuple insertion
  XLOG_RECNO_UPDATE_INPLACE   (0x10)  In-place/out-of-place update (new
                                      image only; old image is in the
                                      per-relation UNDO fork, not this
                                      record -- see 3.3)
  XLOG_RECNO_DELETE            (0x20)  Tuple deletion (old image is in
                                      the per-relation UNDO fork, not
                                      this record)
  XLOG_RECNO_DEFRAG            (0x30)  Single-page defragmentation
  XLOG_RECNO_OVERFLOW_WRITE    (0x40)  Overflow record write / link
                                      back-patch
  XLOG_RECNO_COMPRESS          (0x50)  Attribute compression
  XLOG_RECNO_INIT_PAGE         (0x60)  Page initialization
  XLOG_RECNO_CROSS_PAGE_DEFRAG (0x70)  Cross-page tuple move
  XLOG_RECNO_VM_SET            (0x80)  Set visibility map bits
  XLOG_RECNO_VM_CLEAR          (0x90)  Clear visibility map bits
  XLOG_RECNO_LOCK              (0xA0)  Tuple lock
  XLOG_RECNO_CAS_UPDATE        (0xB0)  Same-size CAS in-place update
                                      (byte-range patch fast path)
  XLOG_RECNO_WRITE_DICT        (0xC0)  Compression-dictionary fork page
                                      write (full-page image)
  XLOG_RECNO_MULTI_INSERT      (0xD0)  Batched multi-tuple insert
                                      (COPY / bulk insert fast path)
  XLOG_RECNO_CAS_UPDATE_UNDO   (0xE0)  CAS in-place update folded with
                                      its per-relation UNDO before-image
                                      into one WAL record

2.2 Key Differences from Heap WAL
-----------------------------------

  - UPDATE logs only the NEW tuple image.  The old (pre-update) tuple
    image lives exclusively in the relation's own per-relation UNDO fork
    (RelUndo, see "RelUndo: the per-relation UNDO fork" in section 3.3),
    written via a separate RELUNDO_UPDATE record
    (or folded into one combined record by XLOG_RECNO_CAS_UPDATE_UNDO on
    the CAS fast path).  It is not duplicated into the main-fork WAL
    record, so WAL volume per UPDATE is dominated by the new tuple size
    plus the (full before-image) UNDO record, not
    a flat 2x multiplier.
  - DML records carry uint64 commit_ts fields (old_commit_ts /
    new_commit_ts on UPDATE; commit_ts on INSERT/DELETE) for page-level
    commit-ts bookkeeping.  The tuple header itself carries t_xmin and
    t_xmax for visibility (see "Tuple Format" in README).
  - TIDs are stable across updates (no HOT chain WAL records needed).
  - DELETE sets RECNO_TUPLE_DELETED flag rather than LP_DEAD, and
    marks the page with RECNO_PAGE_DEFRAG_NEEDED for later reclamation.

2.3 CAS and Folded UNDO Fast Paths
------------------------------------

XLOG_RECNO_CAS_UPDATE is the same-size in-place UPDATE fast path: only
the byte range that actually changed within the tuple is logged (a
byte-offset + length + replacement bytes), and REDO patches those bytes
directly into the on-page tuple.  XLOG_RECNO_CAS_UPDATE_UNDO folds this
with the accompanying per-relation UNDO before-image write into a single
WAL record covering both the main-fork data page (block 0) and the
UNDO-fork data page (block 1, plus an optional UNDO-fork metapage at
block 2 when the UNDO write starts a fresh UNDO page) -- one XLogInsert
for what would otherwise be two separate records (XLOG_RECNO_CAS_UPDATE
plus a RELUNDO_* record), halving WAL-insert overhead on this hot path.
There is no separate "UNDO path in WAL REDO" branch inside the RECNO
resource manager's redo handlers keyed off page-LSN-vs-record comparison
for restoring an old tuple image from the WAL record itself -- that kind
of restore is not needed because the main-fork record never carries an
old-tuple image to restore from (see 2.2).  Idempotent redo/rollback
replay for the UNDO fork instead uses the RELUNDO_INFO_CLR_APPLIED flag
plus full-page-image mechanism described in section 3.4, which is
distinct from (and does not need) page-LSN comparison.

2.4 Defragmentation WAL
-------------------------

XLOG_RECNO_DEFRAG logs offset mappings for diagnostic purposes (useful
for pg_waldump), but the actual REDO implementation calls
PageRepairFragmentation() which achieves the same result more simply.
The page-level RECNO_PAGE_DEFRAG_NEEDED flag is cleared after compaction.

2.5 Timestamp Monotonicity on Pages
-------------------------------------

Every DML REDO handler uses:
  pd_commit_ts = Max(pd_commit_ts, xlrec->commit_ts)

This ensures page-level timestamps never go backward, which is critical
for MVCC visibility fast paths that use the page timestamp as a
conservative upper bound.

2.6 Read-Side Version Reconstruction (PVS)
---------------------------------------------

Because the WAL record for an in-place UPDATE carries only the new tuple
image (2.2), a snapshot older than the current on-page value cannot be
served from WAL replay or from the page alone.  recno_pvs.c's
RecnoReconstructVisibleVersion() closes this gap on the read side: it
walks the version chain threaded through each tuple's t_verptr field
(RelUndoRecPtr, recno.h) back through the per-relation UNDO fork,
reverse-applying each step's stored diff (or full tuple) until it reaches
an image whose creating xid is visible to the reader's snapshot.  This is
the mechanism that makes RECNO's in-place UPDATE compatible with
Repeatable Read / Serializable snapshots held by readers concurrent with
a later committed UPDATE -- distinct from, and complementary to, the
sLog's own before-image retention (SLogBeforeImage, slog.h) used for
shorter-lived recently-committed lookups.  See section 1.2 for how this
fits into the broader visibility path, and section 3.3 for the fork it
reads.


3. Lock Ordering
=================

3.1 Buffer and Tuple Lock Protocol
------------------------------------

  INSERT:   Buffer lock only (new tuples need no tuple lock)
  UPDATE:   Buffer lock (BUFFER_LOCK_EXCLUSIVE, or SHARE_EXCLUSIVE + a
            per-tuple CAS spin lock on the same-size fast path) taken
            FIRST.  The heavyweight LOCKTAG_TUPLE lock is acquired
            LAZILY -- only after a concurrent-update conflict is
            detected while already holding the buffer lock -- not
            up front.
  DELETE:   Same ordering as UPDATE: buffer lock first; LOCKTAG_TUPLE
            acquired lazily on conflict.

This matches heap's approach: heavyweight tuple locks (RecnoLockTuple /
LOCKTAG_TUPLE) exist to serialize SELECT FOR UPDATE/SHARE lockers and
conflicting concurrent updaters against each other while a backend
sleeps (XactLockTableWait); they are not required for ordinary DML,
which is already serialized by the buffer content lock for the duration
of its own page modification.  See the comment at the top of
recno_tuple_update() / recno_tuple_delete() in recno_operations.c
("the exclusive buffer lock is sufficient to prevent concurrent
modifications -- heavyweight tuple locks ... are only needed for SELECT
FOR UPDATE/SHARE, not for regular DML").

Multiple tuple locks (when actually needed, e.g. SELECT FOR UPDATE
against several rows) are acquired in TID-sorted order to prevent
deadlocks (RecnoLockMultipleTuples).

3.2 Overflow Chain Locking
---------------------------

Spatial locality (multiple overflow records on one page) requires the
pin-reuse pattern:

  1. Pin buffer on first access to a page
  2. Lock/unlock between records on the same page (keep pin)
  3. Release pin only when moving to a different page or at chain end

This prevents the assertion failure in bufmgr.c that occurs when
attempting to lock an already-locked buffer.  The pattern is required
for correctness, not merely an optimization.

Used in:
  - RecnoFetchOverflowColumn() (SELECT path, BUFFER_LOCK_SHARE)
  - RecnoDeleteOverflowChain() (DELETE path, BUFFER_LOCK_EXCLUSIVE)

3.3 Interaction with UNDO-in-WAL
----------------------------------

RECNO's own row-level rollback does NOT go through the cluster-wide UNDO
log.  There are two architecturally distinct UNDO mechanisms in this
tree, and RECNO's DML uses only the first:

  RelUndo (per-relation UNDO fork, src/include/access/relundo.h,
  src/backend/access/undo/relundo*.c): each RECNO relation has its own
  UNDO fork (RELUNDO_FORKNUM).  recno_operations.c reserves space via
  RelUndoReserve() and writes RELUNDO_INSERT / RELUNDO_DELETE /
  RELUNDO_UPDATE / RELUNDO_TUPLE_LOCK records
  directly into this fork (RelUndoFinish() / RelUndoStage()).  On abort,
  RelUndoApplyChain() (relundo_apply.c) walks this fork's chain backward
  and restores before-images -- this is the AM's own "UNDO records are
  written to the cluster-wide UNDO log" claim's actual mechanism, and it
  is per-relation, not cluster-wide.  recno_pvs.c's version-chain walk
  (RecnoReconstructVisibleVersion(), see 2.2) also reads this same fork
  to serve older MVCC snapshots -- RelUndo backs both rollback AND
  read-side version reconstruction for RECNO.

  Cluster-wide UNDO-in-WAL (UNDO_RMID_*, src/include/access/undorecord.h,
  src/backend/access/undo/undoapply.c etc.): a shared, AM-agnostic
  mechanism used by heap, nbtree, and hash for their own generic
  rollback.  RECNO's row DML does not write records here
  (am_supports_undo = true is set for RECNO, but its own row operations
  route through RelUndo, not this path -- see the comment on
  recno_methods.am_supports_undo in recno_handler.c).  RECNO does
  register an UNDO resource manager (UNDO_RMID_RECNO, recno_undo.c)
  purely so that index inserts on a RECNO table can piggyback their own
  index-UNDO records (e.g. NbtreeUndoLogInsert()) onto the table's
  active cluster-wide UNDO context; an index rollback on a RECNO table
  therefore goes through this shared mechanism even though the table's
  own row changes do not.

The remainder of this section (crash-safety pattern, CLR idempotency)
describes RelUndo's own apply path (RelUndoApplyChain() /
RelUndoLogApplyCLR() in relundo_apply.c), which every RECNO row
rollback actually uses:

  1. Open relation with RowExclusiveLock
  2. ReadBuffer -> LockBuffer(EXCLUSIVE) on the target data page(s)
     (up to RELUNDO_APPLY_MAX_DATA_BUFS per record; a run of records
     targeting the same single data page may be folded into one CLR,
     see relundo_apply.c's batching comment)
  3. Physical modification (memcpy restore of the before-image),
     OUTSIDE any WAL critical section (may ereport(ERROR) on corruption)
  4. START_CRIT_SECTION
  5. MarkBufferDirty() on every restored data page and every touched
     UNDO-fork page
  6. Set RELUNDO_INFO_CLR_APPLIED (and RELUNDO_INFO_HAS_CLR) in place on
     each applied record's UNDO-fork page header
  7. XLogInsert(RM_RELUNDO_ID, XLOG_RELUNDO_APPLY), forcing a full-page
     image of every restored data page AND every touched UNDO-fork page
     (REGBUF_STANDARD | REGBUF_FORCE_IMAGE) -- a full-page image is
     required because the physical restoration is an arbitrary in-place
     rewrite with no forward WAL record to re-derive it from (the
     forward DML record only ever carried the *new*, now-aborted value)
  8. PageSetLSN() on every touched buffer
  9. END_CRIT_SECTION
  10. UnlockReleaseBuffer() on data and fork buffers

UNDO record I/O (RelUndoReadRecord) and the physical restore both occur
BEFORE the critical section; only the dirty-marking, flag-setting, and
WAL insert occur inside it.

The intrinsic in-place-UNDO cost (measured, not fixable).  An in-place
UPDATE holds TWO buffer content locks across the WAL insert: the data
page (SHARE_EXCLUSIVE on the CAS fast path, EXCLUSIVE otherwise) AND the
UNDO-fork page that RelUndoReserve()/RelUndoStage() locked to stage the
before-image.  Both must stay locked and pinned when XLogInsert() emits
the single combined record (RecnoXLogCasUpdateUndo), because that record
atomically covers both forks: crash recovery must never see the new data
version durable without its before-image, or vice versa.  Releasing the
UNDO-page lock earlier would split the one atomic record into two and
reintroduce exactly that torn-write class, so the two-lock hold is a
correctness requirement, not an accident.  This is the fundamental cost
of in-place MVCC-over-UNDO: every UPDATE writes the data page AND a
durable before-image under one locked WAL insert, whereas heap leaves the
old version in place and writes no extra durable image.  It is the same
tradeoff zheap and OrioleDB confront.  Concurrent writers to one relation
are spread across RELUNDO_NUM_HEADS (16) independent UNDO tail pages
(slot = MyProcNumber % RELUNDO_NUM_HEADS, see relundo.c) so they do not
all serialize on a single UNDO head-page content lock; the data-page
content lock remains the per-hot-row serialization point, as it is for
heap.  Byte-diff delta UNDO (RELUNDO_INFO_PARTIAL_TUPLE) shrinks the
before-image WAL volume (~30% on a spread-update mix) but does not remove
the two-lock hold.

3.4 Crash During Rollback
---------------------------

If a crash occurs during RelUndo application:
  1. Recovery replays WAL forward, restoring pages via XLOG_RELUNDO_APPLY
     CLR full-page images (both the data page and the UNDO-fork page,
     each carrying RELUNDO_INFO_CLR_APPLIED once its CLR has replayed).
  2. The idempotency mechanism is the RELUNDO_INFO_CLR_APPLIED flag
     stamped directly onto the UNDO-fork page's record header (in place,
     inside the same WAL-protected page) -- NOT a urec_clr_ptr-style
     pointer field written back after the fact.  A record whose fork page
     already carries this flag is skipped by RelUndoApplyChain() on any
     re-drive (worker restart, crash-then-retry).
  3. Because the flag lives on a page that is itself protected by the
     CLR's own full-page image, flag and restored-data-page state always
     advance atomically together during redo -- there is no window where
     one is visible without the other.
  4. Remaining un-flagged UNDO records are applied normally, generating
     new CLRs.
  5. Rollback always completes, even after repeated crashes.

(The cluster-wide UNDO_RMID_* mechanism used by heap/nbtree/hash index
rollback, in contrast, never writes anything back into the UNDO record
itself: undorecord.h's urec_clr_ptr field is always InvalidXLogRecPtr at
every write site and undoapply.c's own comment states it "cannot be
updated after the fact"; that mechanism's idempotency comes from page-LSN
comparison during ordinary WAL redo, not from a CLR-applied flag.  See
the UNDO README in src/backend/access/undo/README for that mechanism's
details -- RECNO's own row rollback does not use it, see 3.3.)

